findings.description:
`run_steps` catches only `StepFailed` and `subprocess.CalledProcessError`, and `main()` writes the report only after `run_steps` returns — there is no `try/finally`. Several step bodies can raise exceptions outside that pair, each of which would abort the run with a traceback and no report:
- an **empty** (but existing) log file makes `.splitlines()[-1]` raise `IndexError` in `run_health`, `step_weekly` (`scripts/cutover_227_tick_backup.py#step_weekly`) and `step_production`. Note the existing `if log.exists() else ""` guards only non-existence, not emptiness, so a health job that died between creating and writing the log hits this;
- `conf_sums` in `scripts/cutover_227_host.py#conf_sums` uses `next(...)` with no default, so a `pg_lsclusters` shape change raises `StopIteration`;
- `CRON_FILE.read_text()` / `.env` reads raise `FileNotFoundError`/`PermissionError` before any step runs.

This contradicts the module docstring ("the first failure stops the run and the report is still written to `project-documents/user/notes/<date>-227-cutover.md`") and slice 227 TD8 step 13 ("stops at the first failed step … and still writes the report"). The failure that loses the report is precisely the unforeseen one the report exists to explain. Wrapping the step dispatch in a broader handler (or writing the report in a `finally`) restores the documented contract.
